# Route learner-facing, latency-sensitive Celery tasks off the default queue.
#
# Why this is needed: every routing key below resolves to DEFAULT_PRIORITY_QUEUE
# upstream (lms/envs/common.py), so activation emails, gradebook recalculation
# and certificate awards all queue behind whatever bulk work is running.
# Measured on prod: edx.lms.core.default held 9,594 messages while
# edx.lms.core.high and .high_mem sat at 0 — that is what produced the ~4 hour
# gradebook lag and 1-3 hour email delays learners reported.
#
# Safe with a single worker: tutor's LMS_WORKER_COMMAND default already passes
#   --queues=edx.lms.core.default,edx.lms.core.high,edx.lms.core.high_mem
# so one worker consumes all three and nothing is orphaned. Identical in the
# docker (local/docker-compose.prod.yml) and k8s (k8s/deployments.yml) paths,
# so stage and prod behave the same.
#
# What this does NOT give you: strict priority. One worker pool serving three
# queues means Celery round-robins between them, so a high task does not
# preempt an in-flight default task. It does mean a deep default queue no
# longer sits in front of a shallow high queue. For true priority, add a
# separate worker deployment consuming only edx.lms.core.high.
#
# Lives in tutor-indigo because indigo is the one plugin enabled in BOTH the
# stage and prod deployment workflows; this is platform config rather than
# theming, so move it to a dedicated plugin if one is ever created.

# Single-learner subsection regrade — the task behind the gradebook lag.
SINGLE_LEARNER_COURSE_REGRADE_ROUTING_KEY = HIGH_PRIORITY_QUEUE

# award_course_certificate / award_program_certificates / revoke_*.
PROGRAM_CERTIFICATES_ROUTING_KEY = HIGH_PRIORITY_QUEUE

# send_grade_to_credentials.
CREDENTIALS_GENERATION_ROUTING_KEY = HIGH_PRIORITY_QUEUE

# ACE covers most learner email (set-password, verification, schedules).
# Upstream hardcodes this to 'edx.lms.core.default' in
# openedx/core/djangoapps/ace_common/settings/common.py.
ACE_ROUTING_KEY = HIGH_PRIORITY_QUEUE

# send_activation_email has no routing key upstream at all, so it falls through
# to CELERY_DEFAULT_QUEUE. Route it explicitly.
EXPLICIT_QUEUES.update({
    "openedx.core.djangoapps.user_authn.tasks.send_activation_email": {
        "queue": HIGH_PRIORITY_QUEUE},
})

# POLICY_CHANGE_GRADES_ROUTING_KEY is deliberately left on the default queue:
# recalculate_course_and_subsection_grades_for_user is bulk work, and bulk work
# is exactly what should stay isolated from interactive traffic.
